### Phase C — auth. Grown by S13 (register/login/me), S14 (refresh/logout),
### S16 (guest). Run the blocks top to bottom: each one leaves cookies the next
### one needs.
###
### Why no X-CSRF-Token header anywhere below: the CSRF check only demands the
### double-submit token from requests that carry an `Origin` — i.e. from
### browsers, the only clients with an ambient cookie jar to ride (07 §5.4).
### A REST client sends no Origin, so it is exempt by construction, not by a
### development-only switch.

@host = http://localhost:3000
@api = {{host}}/api/v1
@email = mehrang@test.dev
@password = correct-horse-battery

### ── S13 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Register. → 201, plus `access` and `refresh` Set-Cookie headers.
### Look at the refresh cookie: Path=/api/v1/auth, not `/`. It is not attached
### to the hundreds of ordinary API calls that have no business seeing it.
# @name register
POST {{api}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}",
  "displayName": "Mehrang"
}

### The same email again → 409 EMAIL_TAKEN. Then open Studio and confirm there
### is still exactly ONE user, ONE preferences row, and three wallets — the
### whole registration is one transaction, so a rejected duplicate leaves
### nothing behind.
POST {{api}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "some-other-password",
  "displayName": "Impostor"
}

### Case is not a second account — the email is normalised. → 409.
POST {{api}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "MEHRANG@Test.DEV",
  "password": "some-other-password",
  "displayName": "Impostor"
}

### ★ A weak password is refused by the shared Zod schema — the same schema the
### S39 sign-up form will import. → 400 with fieldErrors.password.
POST {{api}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "weak@test.dev",
  "password": "short",
  "displayName": "Weak"
}

### ★ You cannot make yourself an admin: `role` is not in the schema, and the
### schema is strict. → 400 naming the unknown key.
POST {{api}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "sneaky@test.dev",
  "password": "correct-horse-battery",
  "displayName": "Sneaky",
  "role": "ADMIN"
}

### Impersonation is refused. "H0st", "Adm1n", "A d m i n" all normalise onto
### the reserved word. → 400 errors.displayNameReserved.
POST {{api}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "fake-host@test.dev",
  "password": "correct-horse-battery",
  "displayName": "H0st"
}

### Log in. → 200 and a fresh cookie pair.
# @name login
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### ★ A wrong password. Compare this response to the next one, body **and**
### response time: an unknown email still pays for one argon2 verification, so
### neither the payload nor the timing tells an attacker which emails exist.
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "definitely-not-it"
}

### An unknown email. Identical 401.
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "nobody-at-all@test.dev",
  "password": "definitely-not-it"
}

### Send the wrong-password block five times in a row and this is what you get:
### 429 with retryAfterMs — 5 attempts / 15 min, per email AND per IP (07 §5.3).
### A correct password is throttled too, or the lockout would be a suggestion.

### Who am I? Cookie only — no Authorization header anywhere in this file.
GET {{api}}/auth/me
Accept: application/json

### ── S14 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Rotate. → 200, and both cookies are replaced. In Studio, the old
### RefreshToken row now has revokedAt and replacedById set, and the new row
### shares its familyId.
# @name refresh
POST {{api}}/auth/refresh

### ★ Now replay a token you already rotated. The clean way to see it: copy the
### `refresh=...` value from the register response above, paste it below, and
### send. → 401 TOKEN_REUSED — and the whole family dies, including the token
### that was valid a second ago. Re-run `/auth/me` and `/auth/refresh`: both
### 401. We cannot tell the thief from the victim, so both log in again.
###
### That containment is the entire point of S14. Confirm the ALERT-severity
### BAD_TOKEN row in SecurityEvent.
POST {{api}}/auth/refresh
Cookie: refresh=PASTE_AN_ALREADY_ROTATED_TOKEN_HERE

### A token nobody issued → 401 UNKNOWN_TOKEN, plus a BAD_TOKEN audit row.
POST {{api}}/auth/refresh
Cookie: refresh=this-was-never-issued

### Log out: revokes the family, clears every cookie. Idempotent — send it
### twice, both 200. "End my session" has one acceptable outcome.
POST {{api}}/auth/logout

### After logout: 401.
GET {{api}}/auth/me
Accept: application/json

### ── S16 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Play with no account. SEEDDEMO is minted by `npm run seed`; S19 mints real
### ones. → 201 with { identity: { kind: "guest", tableId }, redirectTo }.
### ★ Note what is NOT in the request: a tableId. The invite decides which
### table this identity is good for — a caller-supplied table would be a
### privilege-escalation primitive (07 §5.1).
# @name guest
POST {{api}}/auth/guest
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "inviteCode": "SEEDDEMO",
  "displayName": "Sara"
}

### → {"kind":"guest","tableId":"..."} — and no User row was created.
GET {{api}}/auth/me
Accept: application/json

### ★ THE assertion of S16. Take any *other* table id from Studio and put it
### below. → 403 GUEST_TABLE_BINDING, plus an ALERT SEAT_IMPERSONATION row in
### SecurityEvent.
###
### Since S18 this runs against the real route: `GET /tables/:id` carries the
### same `enforceGuestBinding` middleware, so the dev-only `/_probe/table/:id`
### stand-in is gone. `requests/tables.http` mints a second table for you.
GET {{api}}/tables/PASTE_A_DIFFERENT_TABLE_ID
Accept: application/json

### The guest's own table → 200, with the seat map.
GET {{api}}/tables/PASTE_THE_BOUND_TABLE_ID
Accept: application/json

### Unknown, expired and revoked codes are deliberately indistinguishable —
### identical 410 INVITE_EXPIRED for all three, so probing codes yields no
### signal (07 §5.2).
POST {{api}}/auth/guest
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "inviteCode": "NOSUCHCD",
  "displayName": "Sara"
}

### ── S12: the boundary, visible ──────────────────────────────────────────────

### Missing required field → 400 with fieldErrors naming it.
POST {{api}}/_probe
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### ★ "5" is NOT coerced into 5. A coercing boundary turns a client bug into a
### server-side type confusion three layers down.
POST {{api}}/_probe
Content-Type: application/json

{ "n": "5" }

### An unknown key is rejected, not quietly dropped — that is how a misspelled
### field becomes a setting which silently never applied.
POST {{api}}/_probe
Content-Type: application/json

{ "n": 5, "sneaky": true }

### Valid. → 200, echoing the *parsed* value.
POST {{api}}/_probe
Content-Type: application/json

{ "n": 5, "label": "hello" }

### ★ A browser-shaped request without the CSRF header → 403 CSRF_FAILED.
### This is the forged-request shape: the cookie is sent, but a cross-origin
### page cannot read it to build the header.
POST {{api}}/_probe
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: http://localhost:5173

{ "n": 5 }

### A foreign origin → 403 ORIGIN_MISMATCH, before the token is even consulted.
POST {{api}}/_probe
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: https://evil.test

{ "n": 5 }

### helmet's headers, on every response.
GET {{host}}/health
Accept: application/json

### ★ Still 404. There is no admin surface on the public port, and /metrics is
### an admin route from S49 (12 §11.1).
GET {{api}}/metrics
Accept: application/json
